    "description": "Iran’s population is distributed unevenly among provinces and between urban and rural settlements. Census geography provides a useful historical baseline, but population totals, urbanization, and population density describe different aspects of that distribution.",
    "tags": ["Population geography", "Census", "Urbanization", "Regional differences"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:45:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:45:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Population Distribution in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s uneven population geography through provincial concentration, urban and rural residence, and the limits of census comparisons."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "provincial-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A population concentrated unevenly",
        "content": "A population map answers a different question from a map of Iran’s administrative divisions: how many people actually live within each territory? Stanford’s analysis of the 2016 census identifies Tehran, Razavi Khorasan, Esfahan, Fars, and Khuzestan as the most populous provinces. Ilam, Semnan, and Kohgiluyeh and Boyer Ahmad were among the least populous. These are historical census comparisons, rather than a verified ranking for the editorial cutoff. [s1]\n\nProvince names also require care. Tehran province is a larger administrative territory than Tehran city. A provincial population should therefore never be presented as the population of the capital itself. Similarly, comparing provinces by total residents does not establish which has the highest density: the area included in each boundary also matters.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "urban-rural-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Urbanization varies across the country",
        "content": "The same census-based analysis identifies Qom, Tehran, Alborz, and Esfahan as provinces with relatively small rural population shares. Sistan and Baluchestan, Golestan, and Hormozgan had relatively large rural shares. An account centered exclusively on the capital would consequently miss important differences in settlement geography. [s1]\n\nUrban share and provincial population are separate measures. A province may have most of its residents in officially designated urban settlements without being among the country’s most populous territories. Conversely, a substantial rural population can coexist with important cities. Read the urban–rural classification alongside a map of individual settlements rather than treating either category as a uniform landscape."
        ,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "water-and-settlement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Water helps explain settlement locations",
        "content": "Administrative totals describe the pattern; historical water infrastructure helps explain some of its local detail. UNESCO’s account of the Persian qanat describes underground channels that convey groundwater by gravity from aquifers toward settlements and agricultural land. A qanat is thus both a water supply system and part of the historical geography of habitation in arid Iran. [s2]\n\nUNESCO explicitly connects settlement distribution on the inner plateau’s alluvial fans—sediment deposits spreading outward from mountain valleys—with qanat networks. This does not establish that water alone explains modern provincial concentration. It does show why apparently dry landscapes can contain established agricultural settlements, and why a map of rainfall alone cannot describe the geography of permanent habitation. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read changes against their reporting geography",
        "content": "Historical comparison needs matching boundaries as well as matching dates. South Khorasan provides a concrete warning: Encyclopaedia Iranica documents additions to the province after its creation, including Ferdows and later Tabas. An increase in residents counted within a changing province cannot automatically be interpreted as growth within a fixed territory. [s3]\n\nThe practical reading sequence is to identify the reporting unit, establish its boundaries, distinguish counts from estimates, and then compare like with like. The evidence assembled here supports a historical introduction; it does not yet support a current national distribution map or a continuous annual account.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
