    "name": "Rural Settlements",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/geography/human-geography/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s historical rural landscapes include compact villages, fortified settlements, and dispersed Caspian farmsteads. Water supply and local settlement definitions help explain these differences, while contemporary rural population estimates measure people rather than the number or condition of villages.",
    "tags": ["Rural geography", "Villages", "Qanat", "Caspian lowlands", "Settlement patterns"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T12:45:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T12:45:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Rural Settlements in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s historical village forms, Caspian farmsteads, qanat-supported settlements, and the limits of rural population statistics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "village-meaning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A village is more than a cluster of buildings",
        "content": "The Persian word deh refers to a village understood as a distinct social and spatial community. Encyclopaedia Iranica distinguishes it from abadi, a broader term for an inhabited place that can also include a town or an isolated farm. This matters when reading historical settlement inventories: lists assembled under different definitions need not count the same kinds of places. [s1]\n\nThe reference article is a historical synthesis, originally published in 1994 and updated in 2013. Its descriptions help interpret inherited rural landscapes; they should not be mistaken for a survey of present-day housing or village services. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-forms",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Compact villages and dispersed farmsteads",
        "content": "Iranica describes compact villages as a widespread historical form. In arid foothill settings, settlements often developed beside canals or qanat outlets, with gardens and fields arranged around the inhabited area. Fortified villages represent another historical form, with dwellings enclosed by defensive walls. [s1]\n\nThe humid Caspian lowlands offer a different pattern. In Gilan and Mazandaran, the article describes mahalla communities made up of scattered farmsteads, with separate buildings serving household and agricultural functions. Its wider lesson is methodological: a rural settlement cannot always be recognized by looking for one compact built-up core. Statistical village boundaries may group dispersed habitation together. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qanat-landscape",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Water infrastructure as part of the settlement",
        "content": "A qanat collects groundwater and carries it through a gently sloping underground tunnel. At its outlet, channels distribute water toward agricultural land. UNESCO’s heritage documentation treats these channels, associated structures, and water-sharing arrangements as parts of a connected system. [s2]\n\nThis supports a useful way of reading an arid rural landscape: follow the relationship between water collection, conveyance, distribution, and cultivation. Buildings alone reveal only part of the settlement’s organization. UNESCO’s account concerns a heritage system and its selected components; it does not demonstrate that every rural community uses a qanat or that every surviving system functions equally well. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qanat-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How a qanat supports an agricultural settlement",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Groundwater in an aquifer\"] -->|\"Collected by\"| B[\"Qanat tunnel\"]\n  B -->|\"Flows by gravity to\"| C[\"Surface outlet\"]\n  C -->|\"Feeds\"| D[\"Distribution channels\"]\n  D -->|\"Supply irrigation water to\"| E[\"Agricultural land\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe water movement through a simplified qanat system. They do not represent a universal layout for Iranian villages.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "population-measure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Counting rural residents is a different task",
        "content": "The World Bank’s rural population indicator uses national definitions of rural residence. Its metadata describe a midyear estimate calculated from total population and urban–rural proportions, drawing on United Nations urbanization estimates. Such annual values are estimates, rather than fresh village-by-village census enumerations. [s3]\n\nA rural population total consequently cannot establish how many villages are inhabited, which settlements have lost services, or how seasonal residence is changing. Those questions require settlement-level evidence. A contemporary account should connect population estimates with an official village inventory and local research before drawing conclusions about abandonment or rural recovery. The historical forms described here provide context for that work, while current conditions remain a research gap.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
